WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.250
Everything so far stops at
the edge of Microsoft 365.

2
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.934
Nothing on BOD 25-01's list
covers infrastructure accounts,

3
00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:11.900
the agency network or
the virtualized estate.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.574
So the whole-agency argument
rests on the Zero Trust documents,

5
00:00:15.574 --> 00:00:21.650
and on the SP 800-53 controls
every system security plan asserts.

6
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.200
Three things get reconciled
before assessing.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.974
One inventory: CM-8 forbids
duplicate accounting of components,

8
00:00:29.974 --> 00:00:34.550
and BOD 23-01 requires asset
discovery every 7 days.

9
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.131
One naming authority,

10
00:00:36.131 --> 00:00:40.875
because each cloud provider and
VMware ships its own private DNS.

11
00:00:40.875 --> 00:00:44.783
And no implicit trust from any
boundary: a site-to-site VPN,

12
00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:50.187
a default virtual network and a flat
virtualized segment are the same mistake.

13
00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:53.974
The hypervisor is a distinct
threat class, so its management

14
00:00:53.974 --> 00:00:58.063
plane is assessed as its own surface,
not inferred from its guests.

15
00:00:58.063 --> 00:01:01.062
And every provider's tool
answers for its own platform.

16
00:01:01.062 --> 00:01:05.474
AWS says its NIST standard
supports a subset of requirements.

17
00:01:05.474 --> 00:01:09.512
Microsoft calls Azure Policy
compliance only a partial view.

18
00:01:09.512 --> 00:01:14.710
FedRAMP certifies the service; the
agency authorizes its own use of it.
